# netbird stack — manual setup

Trimmed self-hosted control plane (`netbird-server` + `dashboard` only — no
routing peer, no external IdP, no coturn). Read `CONTEXT.md` first for why
this differs from the version retired in `a2b33c6`.

## 0. Before you start

- Confirm you're prepared to restore the `netbird.yggdrasil.cfd` DNS record
  (deleted alongside the old stack) pointing at your home public IP/dynamic
  DNS host, same as every other `*.yggdrasil.cfd` record.
- Confirm your router forwards `3478/udp` to this host. STUN can't go
  through NPM (raw UDP) — same carve-out category as `arr`'s gluetun ports.
- `nginx-proxy-manager` must already be up (`edge` network created,
  `./up-all-stacks.sh nginx-proxy-manager` if not).

## 1. Run the official installer

Don't hand-write `docker-compose.yml` or `config.yaml` for this stack —
they come from NetBird's own installer, which knows the current image
schema. Run it *from this directory* so the generated files land here:

```bash
cd ~/homelab/containers/netbird
export NETBIRD_DOMAIN=netbird.yggdrasil.cfd
curl -fsSL https://github.com/netbirdio/netbird/releases/latest/download/getting-started.sh | bash
```

It will prompt three times (domain is pre-answered by the env var above):

| Prompt | Answer |
|---|---|
| "Which reverse proxy will you use?" | `3` (Nginx Proxy Manager) |
| "Is Nginx Proxy Manager running in Docker? ... Docker network" | `edge` |
| "Should container ports be bound to localhost only?" | Enter (default: yes) |

This generates `docker-compose.yml`, `config.yaml`, `dashboard.env`, and
`npm-advanced-config.txt` in this directory, then starts the containers.
`config.yaml` and `dashboard.env` are gitignored — never commit them.

## 2. Trim the compose file

Done — `docker-compose.yml` in this directory has already had the
`127.0.0.1`-bound `ports:` entries removed for `dashboard` and
`netbird-server` (redundant once they're on `edge`); only `3478:3478/udp`
remains, with a comment explaining why. Applied via `docker compose up -d`.

## 3. Configure NPM

**One** proxy host, not two — the dashboard is the default/catch-all
target, and the advanced config's `location` blocks carve out the specific
gRPC/API/websocket paths to `netbird-server` on the same domain.

**Proxy Hosts → Add Proxy Host**:
- Domain: `netbird.yggdrasil.cfd`
- Forward Hostname: `netbird-dashboard`, Port: `80`, scheme `http`
- Block Common Exploits: enabled
- **SSL tab**: request a new Let's Encrypt certificate, force SSL, **enable
  HTTP/2 Support** (required — gRPC won't work without it)
- **Advanced tab**: paste the contents of `npm-advanced-config.txt` (routes
  `/relay`, `/ws-proxy/`, the gRPC service paths, `/api`, and `/oauth2` to
  `netbird-server:80`; everything else falls through to the dashboard)

## 4. Sanity-check config.yaml

`getting-started.sh`'s combined config template sets
`server.reverseProxy.trustedHTTPProxies` to its built-in Traefik's static
IP by default, which doesn't apply here since NPM is the proxy. Open
`config.yaml`, check that section, and point it at NPM's `edge`-network IP
(`docker inspect nginx-proxy-manager | grep -A5 edge`) or drop the
restriction — otherwise NetBird may not trust NPM's forwarded headers.

## 5. Bring the host's existing netbird client back

This host already has a native `netbird` client installed
(`systemctl status netbird` — it's been running since before the old stack
was removed, currently stuck retrying a dead management URL). Re-point it:

```bash
sudo netbird down
sudo netbird up --management-url https://netbird.yggdrasil.cfd
```

It'll print a URL to open in a browser to authorize the peer (first-run
device flow against the embedded IdP) — or generate a setup key from the
dashboard (**Setup Keys → Add Setup Key**) and use
`netbird up --management-url https://netbird.yggdrasil.cfd --setup-key <key>`
instead if you'd rather not do interactive auth on a headless box.

Confirm it got an overlay IP: `netbird status` (look for the assigned
`100.x.x.x` address).

## 6. Enroll your laptop

Install the NetBird client there (see https://netbird.io/docs/getting-started/installation),
point it at `https://netbird.yggdrasil.cfd` as the management URL, and
authorize it the same way (browser flow or a setup key).

## 7. Restrict SSH to the laptop peer (defense in depth)

Dashboard → **Access Control → Policies**: add a policy allowing only the
laptop's peer/group to reach the host's peer/group on TCP/22. Overlay-network
reachability alone isn't an ACL — anything else you enroll later shouldn't
get SSH just by being on the mesh.

## 8. Verify, and don't forward port 22 anywhere

- `ssh <user>@<host's netbird overlay IP>` from the laptop, over the
  netbird interface, should work.
- Check your router has **no** port-forward for `22/tcp` to this host —
  the whole point of this setup is that SSH never needs one.
- `sshd` currently listens on `0.0.0.0:22` (all interfaces) — that's fine
  as long as nothing forwards WAN traffic to it; the netbird overlay IP is
  reachable on that same all-interfaces bind. For defense in depth beyond
  "no port forward," consider a host firewall rule that only allows
  `22/tcp` from your LAN subnet and the netbird interface (`wt0` or similar
  — check `ip addr` after step 5), rejecting it on the WAN-facing interface
  explicitly.

## Rollback

`docker compose -f docker-compose.yml down`, remove the NPM proxy host(s),
drop the DNS record. `config.yaml`/`dashboard.env` are gitignored so
nothing to un-commit. The native `netbird` client on the host will just go
back to failing to connect (as it is now) until re-pointed or disabled
(`sudo systemctl disable --now netbird`).
